1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,255
There's one animal with a backbone that's known to have algae living inside its cells.

2
00:00:05,255 --> 00:00:06,580
The spotted salamander.

3
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:11,209
Its eggs turn green with algae living in the jelly around each embryo,

4
00:00:11,209 --> 00:00:14,926
and some of those algae move right into the embryo's own cells.

5
00:00:15,426 --> 00:00:17,650
So what if you could do something like that?

6
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:20,077
What if your skin could make food from sunlight?

7
00:00:24,333 --> 00:00:25,515
Here are the rules.

8
00:00:25,515 --> 00:00:30,678
Your skin is packed with chloroplasts, the tiny green solar panels that plants use.

9
00:00:30,678 --> 00:00:34,970
A leaf holds about half a million of them in every square millimeter.

10
00:00:35,470 --> 00:00:40,335
Add sunlight, water and carbon dioxide, and they make sugar for your body.

11
00:00:40,335 --> 00:00:42,110
You can still eat normally.

12
00:00:42,110 --> 00:00:43,688
The sunlight is a bonus.

13
00:00:43,688 --> 00:00:48,027
And to keep things simple, the rest of you stays exactly the same.

14
00:00:48,527 --> 00:00:51,475
Chloroplasts were once free-living bacteria,

15
00:00:51,475 --> 00:00:55,227
swallowed by another cell well over a billion years ago.

16
00:00:55,227 --> 00:01:00,118
They even still carry their own DNA, a leftover from that bacterial past.

17
00:01:00,118 --> 00:01:03,602
Every leaf on Earth still runs on that ancient deal.

18
00:01:04,467 --> 00:01:07,013
That deal is also why you can breathe.

19
00:01:07,013 --> 00:01:09,627
About two point four billion years ago,

20
00:01:09,627 --> 00:01:14,184
tiny photosynthesizing microbes started filling the air with oxygen.

21
00:01:14,684 --> 00:01:15,085
Today,

22
00:01:15,085 --> 00:01:20,843
tiny drifting plankton in the ocean make roughly half of the oxygen produced on Earth.

23
00:01:20,843 --> 00:01:24,727
Every breath you take is, in a way, exhaust from sunlight.

24
00:01:25,567 --> 00:01:27,316
So first, you'd turn green.

25
00:01:27,316 --> 00:01:32,240
Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light strongly, and uses less of the green.

26
00:01:32,740 --> 00:01:35,967
So green is what bounces back to everyone else's eyes.

27
00:01:38,733 --> 00:01:40,008
Seven in the morning.

28
00:01:40,008 --> 00:01:44,015
You step outside, sunlight hits your skin, and for the first time,

29
00:01:44,015 --> 00:01:46,321
your body starts making its own sugar.

30
00:01:46,821 --> 00:01:49,812
Most of it would come in the hours around noon.

31
00:01:49,812 --> 00:01:54,649
At dawn and dusk, when the light is low and weak, you'd make almost nothing.

32
00:01:54,649 --> 00:01:59,359
On a gray, overcast day, you'd make only a fraction of a sunny day's haul.

33
00:01:59,359 --> 00:02:00,759
So how much, in total?

34
00:02:00,759 --> 00:02:01,904
Let's do the math.

35
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:06,587
At midday, under a clear sky,

36
00:02:06,587 --> 00:02:10,890
sunlight delivers about a thousand watts onto every square meter.

37
00:02:10,890 --> 00:02:14,400
Your skin covers about one point eight square meters,

38
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:17,578
but only about half of it faces the Sun at once.

39
00:02:18,078 --> 00:02:19,069
Now efficiency.

40
00:02:19,069 --> 00:02:19,731
In theory,

41
00:02:19,731 --> 00:02:24,821
the best plants can store only about five or six percent of sunlight as food.

42
00:02:24,821 --> 00:02:29,251
Even the best real crops manage two or three percent over a season,

43
00:02:29,251 --> 00:02:32,028
and typical plants, more like one percent.

44
00:02:32,528 --> 00:02:34,680
And the Sun isn't overhead all day.

45
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:38,678
Average in the mornings, the evenings, the clouds and the nights,

46
00:02:38,678 --> 00:02:42,245
and your green skin might collect around one or two watts.

47
00:02:42,245 --> 00:02:45,198
That's less than a small phone charger puts out.

48
00:02:46,033 --> 00:02:49,663
Your body runs on about a hundred watts, all day, every day.

49
00:02:50,163 --> 00:02:58,888
Oxford plant ecologist Lindsay Turnbull estimated photosynthetic skin would cover about one percent of a person's daily energy.

50
00:02:58,888 --> 00:03:01,910
In food terms, that's about a cracker a day.

51
00:03:02,410 --> 00:03:03,891
To run on sunlight alone,

52
00:03:03,891 --> 00:03:07,445
she figured you'd need about a tennis court's worth of skin.

53
00:03:07,445 --> 00:03:11,235
That's around a hundred and fifty times more skin than you have.

54
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:14,187
So how do plants get away with it?

55
00:03:14,187 --> 00:03:16,458
They're built completely differently.

56
00:03:16,458 --> 00:03:20,141
A tree spreads its leaves out into a huge, thin solar array.

57
00:03:20,641 --> 00:03:24,908
And it doesn't walk around, doesn't keep its body warm, and doesn't run a brain.

58
00:03:25,408 --> 00:03:27,403
Even then, plants pay a price.

59
00:03:27,403 --> 00:03:32,789
Sugar-making needs water, and leaves lose huge amounts of it through their pores.

60
00:03:32,789 --> 00:03:33,321
The U.S.

61
00:03:33,321 --> 00:03:40,902
Geological Survey says a large oak tree can give off about a hundred and fifty thousand liters of water in a year.

62
00:03:41,767 --> 00:03:44,850
Your brain is only about two percent of your body weight,

63
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:47,447
but it uses about twenty percent of your energy.

64
00:03:47,947 --> 00:03:52,867
Even at high noon, your green skin would make less power than your brain alone uses.

65
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:57,826
A year in, the small stuff adds up.

66
00:03:57,826 --> 00:04:01,515
Anyone working outdoors gets a free snack every sunny day.

67
00:04:01,515 --> 00:04:04,186
Sunny countries get more than cloudy ones.

68
00:04:04,186 --> 00:04:07,175
And far from the equator, winter would be lean,

69
00:04:07,175 --> 00:04:09,655
with the Sun low in the sky for months.

70
00:04:10,155 --> 00:04:12,408
Clothes become a strange trade-off.

71
00:04:12,408 --> 00:04:15,369
Every shirt is a cover over your solar panels.

72
00:04:15,369 --> 00:04:18,717
Summer fashion would get very short, and very green.

73
00:04:19,217 --> 00:04:23,458
But photosynthesizing well means lots of sunlight on bare skin.

74
00:04:23,458 --> 00:04:27,363
And sunlight carries ultraviolet light, which damages DNA.

75
00:04:27,363 --> 00:04:32,682
Plants deal with that every day, using protective pigments and constant repair.

76
00:04:32,682 --> 00:04:35,577
Your green skin would need the same tricks.

77
00:04:36,077 --> 00:04:38,363
It would also need carbon dioxide.

78
00:04:38,363 --> 00:04:42,665
Leaves are covered in tiny pores called stomata that let air in.

79
00:04:42,665 --> 00:04:45,287
Your skin would need something similar.

80
00:04:45,787 --> 00:04:47,825
And green skin only makes sugar.

81
00:04:47,825 --> 00:04:52,092
Plants get nitrogen and minerals from the soil through their roots.

82
00:04:52,092 --> 00:04:55,849
You'd still need real food to build proteins, and at night,

83
00:04:55,849 --> 00:04:58,014
your skin would simply switch off.

84
00:04:58,867 --> 00:05:02,673
To really live on sunlight, you'd have to cut your energy bill.

85
00:05:02,673 --> 00:05:04,909
Cold-blooded animals already do that.

86
00:05:05,409 --> 00:05:10,289
A resting reptile uses only about a tenth of the energy of a mammal its size,

87
00:05:10,289 --> 00:05:12,951
because it doesn't burn fuel to stay warm.

88
00:05:13,451 --> 00:05:17,115
So picture a slow, cold-blooded human, basking on a rock.

89
00:05:17,115 --> 00:05:20,009
Even then, sunlight-skin wouldn't come close.

90
00:05:22,767 --> 00:05:24,916
Now give evolution a million years,

91
00:05:24,916 --> 00:05:28,478
and imagine the people who soak up the most light do best.

92
00:05:28,978 --> 00:05:30,211
Bodies get flatter.

93
00:05:30,211 --> 00:05:30,600
Wider.

94
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:31,119
Thinner.

95
00:05:31,119 --> 00:05:33,779
Big flaps of skin spread out like leaves.

96
00:05:33,779 --> 00:05:35,012
Less moving around.

97
00:05:35,012 --> 00:05:35,921
Less thinking.

98
00:05:36,421 --> 00:05:39,760
Push that far enough, and you basically turn into a plant.

99
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:41,661
And plants already won that game.

100
00:05:41,661 --> 00:05:45,346
They don't hunt for food, because their food falls from the sky.

101
00:05:45,846 --> 00:05:49,018
Funny thing is, some plants went the other way.

102
00:05:49,018 --> 00:05:53,000
Rafflesia, which grows one of the biggest flowers on Earth,

103
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,160
gave up photosynthesis entirely.

104
00:05:55,160 --> 00:06:00,763
It lives as a parasite inside a vine, and may even have lost its chloroplast genes.

105
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:03,647
Except some animals found a middle path.

106
00:06:04,147 --> 00:06:05,335
Corals are animals.

107
00:06:05,335 --> 00:06:12,210
The tiny algae living inside them can hand over as much as ninety percent of the food they make from sunlight.

108
00:06:12,210 --> 00:06:16,335
That's how corals build giant reefs in clear, nutrient-poor water.

109
00:06:16,335 --> 00:06:19,460
The Great Barrier Reef, built on that partnership,

110
00:06:19,460 --> 00:06:22,960
is the largest structure made by living things on Earth.

111
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:24,585
You can see it from space.

112
00:06:25,085 --> 00:06:27,619
The algae also give corals their colors.

113
00:06:27,619 --> 00:06:30,280
Lose the algae, and the coral turns white.

114
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:31,737
That's coral bleaching.

115
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:35,287
Giant clams farm algae in their fleshy mantle,

116
00:06:35,287 --> 00:06:39,375
which soaks up about ninety-five percent of the sunlight that hits it.

117
00:06:39,875 --> 00:06:45,087
And the green hydra, a tiny freshwater animal, keeps algae inside its cells.

118
00:06:45,087 --> 00:06:46,870
The algae pay it in sugar.

119
00:06:46,870 --> 00:06:48,585
It pays them in nitrogen.

120
00:06:49,085 --> 00:06:52,173
There's even a jellyfish that lives upside down.

121
00:06:52,173 --> 00:06:55,904
The upside-down jellyfish lies bell-first on the seafloor,

122
00:06:55,904 --> 00:06:56,869
arms facing up,

123
00:06:56,869 --> 00:07:01,179
so the algae living in those arms get as much sunlight as possible.

124
00:07:02,033 --> 00:07:04,410
Then there's a sea slug that steals.

125
00:07:04,410 --> 00:07:06,325
Elysia chlorotica eats algae,

126
00:07:06,325 --> 00:07:10,154
but keeps their chloroplasts working inside its own cells,

127
00:07:10,154 --> 00:07:13,191
and can go nine months or more without eating.

128
00:07:13,691 --> 00:07:18,894
A leaf-shaped animal, just a few centimeters long, running on stolen solar panels.

129
00:07:18,894 --> 00:07:23,526
Although scientists still argue about how much the sunlight really helps.

130
00:07:24,026 --> 00:07:26,456
Some of its relatives are even stranger.

131
00:07:26,456 --> 00:07:30,587
They can drop their entire body, and the head grows a whole new one,

132
00:07:30,587 --> 00:07:32,470
heart included, in a few weeks.

133
00:07:33,333 --> 00:07:34,586
And our salamander?

134
00:07:34,586 --> 00:07:37,355
Inside the egg, the algae give off oxygen,

135
00:07:37,355 --> 00:07:40,849
and in return they get the embryo's waste to feed on.

136
00:07:40,849 --> 00:07:43,551
Eggs with algae hatch earlier and bigger.

137
00:07:43,551 --> 00:07:47,111
But once the larvae grow, most of the algae disappear.

138
00:07:47,611 --> 00:07:52,419
Even nature's best example uses sunlight as a head start, not a meal plan.

139
00:07:53,267 --> 00:07:58,044
So photosynthetic skin would get you a green tint and about a cracker a day.

140
00:07:58,044 --> 00:07:59,112
Not a revolution.

141
00:07:59,112 --> 00:08:00,118
But not nothing.

142
00:08:00,118 --> 00:08:03,827
And it would make you, quite literally, a little bit plant.

143
00:08:04,327 --> 00:08:07,572
Would you take green skin for a free snack every sunny day?

144
00:08:07,572 --> 00:08:08,892
Tell me in the comments.
